# The ferry town's places on the town plan, named from what happens in each, and there is.
lesson language-the-town v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=5 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "[[town.in-the-town|cap]]"
  goal "Name the places in a town in [[language.name]] and say what there is."

  look {
    scene 28x23 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        townplan plan places=["[[town.the-cafe]]", "[[town.the-market]]", "[[town.the-park]]", "[[town.the-beach]]", "[[town.the-museum]]", "[[town.the-station]]", "[[town.the-square]]"]
      }
    }
    say """
      This is the ferry town. There is a café, a market, a park, a beach, a museum, a station and a
      square. To say there is, say [[town.there-is-a-beach]], there is a beach. To say there is not:
      [[town.there-is-no-library]], there is no library.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.the-town-name k=4
    }
    show language.the-town-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.the-town-meaning k=3 hard-k=5
    show language.the-town-write k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show language.the-town-name k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=6
    show language.the-town-match k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=3
    show language.the-town-write k=5 easy-k=4 hard-k=6
    show language.the-town-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.the-town-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.the-town-write k=3
    }
  }
  language es {
    try stars=2 {
      show language.es-the-town-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    }
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.the-town-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[town.the-beach|cap]], [[town.the-market]], [[town.the-park]], [[town.the-station]], [[town.the-harbour]], [[town.the-square]]. [[town.there-is-a-beach]] There is a beach."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is two places with names that look alike taken for each other. The
    places are asked from what happens in them rather than from a picture, which is a reading
    question as much as a word question, and the town plan in the look and the three-star question
    is the ferry town. The listening question is a dictation: read the sentence aloud from this
    sheet, where it prints as Read aloud, or let the device play it. It is marked word by word, and
    a missing accent counts there, while the words typed on their own in this lesson are taken
    without their accents. If the child is stuck, walk the plan with a finger, naming each place.
    Off the page, name five places in your own town in the language. It meets England's key stage 2
    languages programme (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7): "describe people, places, things and
    actions orally and in writing"; Japan's grades 3 and 4 foreign language activities: "Situations
    where typical expressions are often used: Greetings, Self-introductions, Shopping, Having meals,
    Asking and giving directions"; and CEFR A2, "Can describe in simple terms aspects of his/her
    background, immediate environment and matters in areas of immediate need". The phrases are in
    [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[town.the-harbour]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[town.the-beach]] and [[town.the-market]] and the words to write are [[town.park]] and [[town.square]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[town.the-square]] and [[town.the-library]] and the words to write are [[town.museum]], [[town.station]] and [[town.harbour]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
